Finished this one up yesterday on NAO. I have ascended all the classes,
and done a few of them geno/poly/wishless, so I decided to give the
pacifist conduct a try. I had really enjoyed doing the protection
racket in previous games, so I thought it might be fun.

I made it to minetown with the starting pet, no problem. Being a gnome
makes this part easy. Nothing special, coaligned altar, bought
protection. Found a magic harp and magic whistle in the mines, my first
lucky finds. Also found a [oMR and "oR from a bones pile, two big
hurdles passed. Used the magic harp to tame the two vampires at Mines'
End. They make pretty good pets. Went back to minetown, the vamps
killed the priest, who offered up another [oMR. Lost one of the
vampires somewhere, the other one cleared sokoban for me and took the
"oR. Then we descended the main dungeon, where he snagged a pair of
speed boots. I felt pretty good about my regenerating, magic protected,
reflecting vampire, so of course he promptly died by attacking blue
jellies.

So I trekked back to the mines, found my original dog, and polytrapped
him into a master mind flayer. Then I spent a couple thousand turns
consolidating all my stashes into one at an altar on lvl 15. There was
a priest there, who got mindflayed into oblivion, offering up yet
another [oMR. (What are the odds for that, anyway, as opposed to a
robe?)

Since I had not yet identified identify, I blessed the most common
scrolls, took off all my armor save a crap helmet, and began testing
them. Found amnesia, destroy armor, charging, charged up my harp,
genocide (not blessed, soldiers please), then a few others, finally one
identify. So I used a magic marker found in the mines to write scrolls
until I got a full inventory ident. I had a ring of conflict, so I felt
pretty confident again.

So naturally I thought I'd find the speed boots, [oMR, and "oR that the
vampire was carrying. Just as we entered the room, all the stuff was
eaten by a gelatinous cube, and then my mmf got killed by the same blue
jellies. Ugh. Stupid. Then I went on tilt and crash dived to the
castle, without a pet.

I opened the drawbridge, left the moat alone, engraved Elbereth, and
slipped on my ring. After the foyer was mostly clear, I read a scroll
of earth and entered. A rock troll was the sole survivor, so I tamed
him, and he turned out to be an awesome pet. Entering the castle at exp
lvl 3 makes the monsters spawned pretty tame, so the troll had no
trouble clearing it out. Got the wand, 0:1.

Eye of the Aethiopica please.
Gated back to sokoban, down to my stash, recharged the wand, then:

Platinum Yendorian Express Card
blessed figurine of an Archon
magic marker
bag of holding

With the Archon, the rest of the game was pretty easy, except for
Orcus. I genocided all the swallowing monsters, t,w,v and also
P,h,L,R,c (possible due to about three magic markers). I was very
paranoid about levelporters taking my Archon, so I used a crystal ball
+ PYEC to detect most of the traps in Gehennom. And of course, much
polypiling for equipment. Used the Staff on Rodney to get the Archon to
attack him.

I did have a close call with Orcus, when he cursed my unicorn horn,
which I accidentally used without checking, so I ended up
hallucinating. I wielded it to uncurse it, forgetting that you can't
cast when wielding a cursed two-handed weapon. Very dumb. So I waited
for the hallucination to wear off, dropped the Amulet of Yendor, then
gated back to my stash and prayed, and the unihorn was uncursed. When I
went back to get the amulet, Orcus had it. The Archon wouldn't attack
him, even with conflict, so I engraved E on the upstairs and basically
waited for Orcus to summon something that would kill him. Along came
Yeenoghu, who kicked his ass, so I grabbed the amulet and ran.

Archons really do make awesome pets. Mine ended up with +7 Demonbane
and AC -20. I got tired of the constant attack messages, so on the
Astral Plane I put on my blindfold and watched as the enemies just
disappeared, one by one. It was very, very cool. Went to Famine,
paralyzed him, made it to the lawful altar, put on my helm, and I was
done. Thanks for reading.

Erokh, lawful male gnomish Healer

